### I CANNOT SOLVE THIS

This is Erdős Problem 5, an open research problem in number theory. The conjecture asks whether the set of limit points of the normalized prime gaps $(p_{n+1}-p_n)/\log n$ equals $[0,\infty]$. While several partial results are known (Westzynthius showed $\infty$ is a limit point, Goldston–Pintz–Yıldırım showed $0$ is a limit point, Erdős–Ricci showed the limit point set has positive measure, and Hildebrand–Maier showed it contains arbitrarily large finite numbers), the full conjecture that *every* $C \geq 0$ is a limit point remains open and is beyond current mathematical knowledge. I cannot provide a proof.
